│ improved significantly by adding an estimated RA pressure, but LoA remained wide:-29.9 to 26.8, bias-1.5 mmHg (95%   │
│ CI:-3.1 to 0.1). When assessing the agreement between invasive measures of RA pressure (or RVEDP) and                │
│ echocardiographic assessment of RA pressure, there was a negative bias (underestimation), but with wide LoA:-17.9 to │
│ 4.3, bias-6.8 mmHg (95% CI:-7.4 to-6.1). There was also poor agreement between invasive PA systolic pressure and     │
│ echocardiographic estimate based on TR gradient plus RA pressure:-56.8 to 35.9, bias-10.4 mmHg (95% CI:-13.2         │
│ to-7.6). ROC analysis demonstrated a good discriminative power of PAT in identifying patients with amPAP>= 25mmHg    │
│ (AUC= 76.8) and identified an optimal threshold of 75msec (specificity 82.4%, sensitivity 60.8%).Conclusions:        │
│ Neither PR velocity nor PAT can be used alone in predicting PAP in patients with suspected PH. A combination of      │
│ various echocardiographic parameters in well-defined and validated scores is likely to wield a better diagnostic     │
│ power.Oxford University Press"}, {"study_index": 112, "title": "Accuracy of echocardiographic estimates of pulmonary │
│ artery pressures in pulmonary hypertension: insights from the KARUM hemodynamic database", "abstract": "Accurate     │
│ assessment of pulmonary artery (PA) pressures is integral to diagnosis, follow-up and therapy selection in pulmonary │
